          "session_id": "full-g-md-003",
          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My son has been wheezing at night for a month and his blue inhaler is running out fast. Should he be seen urgently, and what does asthma guidance describe about stepping up treatment?",
          "answer": "**Should he be seen urgently?** A fixed symptom check found no emergency pattern, but that does not mean he is fine. A reliever running out fast suggests his asthma is not well controlled, so don't wait for a routine appointment. Contact his doctor or asthma nurse within the next few days to arrange a review (guideline-asthma-diagnosis-and-stepwise-therapy). One of the summaries suggested contacting them today if he is comfortable. Guidance also recommends a review within one week after a flare-up (guideline-asthma-diagnosis-and-stepwise-therapy).\n\n**Call emergency services now if he has any of these** (red-flag-severe-breathing-difficulty; condition-asthma):\n- Hard breathing at rest, or breathing trouble that came on suddenly\n- Trouble speaking in full sentences, or pausing for breath between words\n- Blue or grey lips, tongue, face or fingertips\n- Drowsiness, confusion, agitation or exhaustion\n- Fast breathing, skin pulling in between or under the ribs, grunting, or flaring nostrils\n- Too breathless to feed or talk\n- No improvement after reliever treatment, or symptoms returning within a few hours\n- A chest that has gone quiet. This is a serious sign, not a reassuring one.\n\nI could not find red-flag guidance written specifically for children with asthma, so follow these signs and any asthma action plan his clinician has given you.\n\n**What asthma guidance says about stepping up treatment** (guideline-asthma-diagnosis-and-stepwise-therapy; lifestyle-asthma-adherence):\n- Treatment works in steps. It is adjusted up or down based on how well symptoms are controlled, and it is reviewed rather than fixed.\n- Rising reliever use is a sign that asthma is not under control, not a sign that the reliever is working well.\n- Before stepping up, the clinician should check inhaler technique and whether medicine is being taken as prescribed. Poor technique and missed doses are often the cause of apparent treatment failure.\n- Review is usually 1 to 3 months after starting or changing treatment (guideline-asthma-diagnosis-and-stepwise-therapy).\n- A written action plan, saying what to do when symptoms worsen and when to seek help, is recommended. Ask his clinician for one if you don't have it.\n\n**Where the guidance differs:** For mild asthma, global strategy guidance favours an inhaled steroid with formoterol as the reliever. Some national panel guidance says a short-acting reliever alongside a regular controller remains appropriate. I can't tell which applies to your son. That is for his clinician to decide.\n\nThe sources I found are mainly about adults and adolescents and do not say which step your son should be on. His clinician can check his technique and symptoms in person.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
